---
import AfternoonLife from "../../components/graph/AfternoonLife.astro";
import PaperTrip from "../../components/graph/PaperTrip.astro";
import RequestLoop from "../../components/graph/RequestLoop.astro";
import { NOTEBOOK } from "../../i18n/ui";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  lang="en"
  page="flows"
  eyebrow="Requests, state and model calls"
  title="How it flows"
  description="Who is allowed to ask whom, what happens to an activity between being invented and being over, and what a sheet of paper does on its round trip."
>
  <p class="lede">
    Who is allowed to ask whom, what happens to an activity between being invented and being over,
    and what a sheet of paper does on its round trip.
  </p>

  <h2>Who asks whom</h2>

  <p>
    The hub initiates every network exchange with the house. A parent action changes state in the
    panel, and the hub receives that state on its next poll. Cloud-to-house data therefore travels
    inside replies to outbound HTTPS requests from the hub.
  </p>

  <RequestLoop
    title="Who asks whom"
    desc="The house contains a hub, two e-paper displays, a button, a printer and a scanner. The hub calls the panel API in Azure and receives work in the response. Inside Azure, the API sends model work through the router and Content Safety to Microsoft Foundry. A parent's approval is stored by the panel until the hub polls again."
    caption="Both exchanges across the boundary start at the hub. The panel persists a parent's decision; the next poll carries it into the house."
    house="The house"
    cloud="Azure, EU region"
    parent={{ title: "A parent, in a browser", sub: ["approves, and sets the hours"] }}
    writes="writes a row"
    displays={{ title: "e-paper displays", sub: ["a finished 1-bit bitmap"] }}
    button={{ title: "a button", sub: ["answered in the reply"] }}
    hub={{
      title: "Hub",
      sub: ["clock · sealing keys · print queue", "the only thing that starts work"],
    }}
    paper={{ title: "printer · scanner", sub: ["one A4 sheet at a time"] }}
    shows="shows"
    press="a press"
    sheet="out, and back in"
    api={{ title: "panel + API", sub: ["stores decisions for the next poll"] }}
    router={{ title: "model router", sub: ["the only module allowed a model SDK"] }}
    gate={{ title: "Content Safety", sub: ["mints the screened payload"] }}
    foundry={{ title: "Microsoft Foundry", sub: ["language · vision · image"] }}
    asks={["asks,", "on its own clock"]}
    answers={["answers,", "inside the reply"]}
  />

  <p class="note">
    Every inference call uses Microsoft Foundry through the router. An import-level test keeps model
    runtimes out of the device packages. This gives the system one inference path and one set of
    deployed models; reading a returned page therefore requires cloud connectivity.
  </p>

  <h2>The life of an activity</h2>

  <p>
    The initial document contains every moment, three lengths for each, four levels of help and a
    route to the ending. The execution service selects from that graph as time and responses change.
    A planned <code>ask</code> can send a returned page to the API and receive a continuation in the
    same format.
  </p>

  <AfternoonLife
    title="The life of an activity"
    desc="The hub requests an activity. The container generates, validates and screens it, then waits for parent approval. The hub retrieves approved activities. A returned reading selects a moment or requests a checked continuation without another parent approval."
    caption="The left return edge is a repair after validation. The right return edge is a planned continuation based on the page that came back."
    idea={{ title: "The house requests an activity", sub: ["preferences · optional brief"] }}
    devise={{
      title: "A model writes the whole activity",
      sub: ["moments, help and ways out"],
    }}
    checks={{
      title: "Format, checks and safety",
      sub: ["the judge is a separate reading"],
    }}
    waits={{ title: "The API awaits approval", sub: ["the parent reads the activity"] }}
    begins={{ title: "The house begins it", sub: ["only if the whole length still fits"] }}
    moment={{ title: "A moment runs", sub: ["say · hand over · collect"] }}
    cameBack={{ title: "What came back", sub: ["marks, or blank"] }}
    ends={{ title: "It ends on the object", sub: ["the same ending, however it went"] }}
    ask={{
      title: "ask",
      sub: [
        "the house sends descriptions of ink",
        "the container checks the next segment",
        "without a second parent approval",
      ],
    }}
    refused="refused"
    passes="passes"
    approving="the next device poll"
    nextMoment="the next moment"
    askEdge="ask"
    continuation="a continuation, in the same format"
    closes="it closes"
  />

  <p>
    Generation permits a format repair and a separate checks repair; continuation has no repair. The hub obeys the hours and daily allowance. The timer starts the way out on its first check after the thirty-minute threshold. Consecutive non-collection moments do not wait their declared durations.
  </p>

  <h2>The paper round trip</h2>

  <p>
    The paper contains only the page a person reads and writes on. The vision call compares the
    returned scan with the original image, so the page needs no machine-readable registration layer.
  </p>

  <PaperTrip
    title="The paper round trip"
    desc="Words that have already passed the safety gate go into one image call, which draws the whole sheet. The sheet is printed and its blank is kept. Somebody writes on it, or does not. The scanner takes it back, both images go to a model, and the answer is either marks or blank. That answer is what the next words are written from."
    caption="The person's action is the only step in this loop that software does not perform."
    words={{ title: "Words", sub: ["already through the safety gate"] }}
    drawn={{ title: "One image call", sub: ["the whole A4 sheet"] }}
    printed={{
      title: "Printed",
      sub: ["the blank is kept: it is what the", "reading is compared against"],
    }}
    person={{ title: "Somebody writes on it", sub: ["or does not"] }}
    scanned={{ title: "Scanner", sub: ["captures the returned sheet"] }}
    read={{ title: "Two images to a model", sub: ["describes the added marks"] }}
    outcome={{ title: "marks · blank", sub: ["descriptions for continuation", "degraded reading: wait"] }}
    feeds="and the next words are written from it"
  />

  <p>
    The branch depends on whether marks were added; the descriptions inform the continuation. Retained profile observations and the development exception are described under <a href="/en/#memory">system memory</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The model-call path</h2>

  <p>
    The normal path selects a method, devises the activity, rereads it and screens its text. Format
    repair and validation repair add calls only after a refusal. Each call has its own prompt, input
    contract and output contract, so a failed result can be traced to the request that produced it.
  </p>

  <figure class="graph">
    <div class="graph-frame" tabindex="0" role="group" aria-labelledby="calls-t">
      <img
        src="/images/calls-en.svg"
        alt="The panel sends method names to the model and receives a form and a move. It then requests the complete activity and receives one JSON document. A format refusal returns the original answer for repair. A structural refusal returns the document with the failed checks for one repair attempt. Finally the document text goes to the safety gate."
        width="833"
        height="721"
      />
    </div>
    <figcaption id="calls-t">
      Method selection, devising, rereading and safety screening form the normal path. The repair
      boxes run only when format parsing or structural validation refuses an answer.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    <a href={NOTEBOOK}>research/officina.ipynb</a> records each request and response at the router.
    The checked-in outputs show the complete call sequence without running the notebook or supplying
    credentials.
  </p>
</Base>
